First Author: Sage
Title: A machine-learning approach to human ex vivo lung perfusion predicts transplantation outcomes and promotes organ utilization
Source number: 43  ￼

Very short summary: This study developed InsighTx, an XGBoost-based machine-learning model for ex vivo lung perfusion (EVLP) assessment, using the largest reported clinical EVLP dataset to date. Five key findings were: (1) 725 eligible clinical EVLP cases from 2008–2022 were included; (2) the overall InsighTx AUROC was 79 ± 3% in training, 75 ± 4% in test dataset 1, and 85 ± 3% in test dataset 2; (3) the model performed especially well for unsuitable lungs, with AUROC up to 95 ± 2%, and for lungs leading to extubation <72 h, with AUROC up to 83 ± 4%; (4) among lungs predicted to have extubation <72 h, the negative predictive value for PGD grade 3 at 72 h was 88%; and (5) in a blinded implementation analysis, InsighTx increased the odds of a favorable transplant decision for suitable lungs by 13-fold and decreased the odds of transplanting unsuitable lungs. Tables 1–4 and Figures 1 and the supplementary decision-analysis figures were directly used for this extraction.  ￼

